# PVAS sandbox netpolicy: bridge-deny
# 
# Drop all outbound traffic from PVAS sandbox containers by default.
# Only ESTABLISHED/RELATED connections are allowed back (for the duration
# of the connection) so that responses to in-container requests work.
#
# This is the strictest policy and is the default for complete audits.
# Use bridge-allow for ad-hoc tool scans that need network (e.g.,
# pulling fresh OSV data); use host for the driver orchestrator.
#
# Applied by tools/pvas_netpolicy.py when a container is launched with
# network_policy="bridge-deny". The iptables chain is named
# PVAS_BRIDGE_DENY_<audit_id>.

# Allow established connections (response to in-container request)
-A PVAS_BRIDGE_DENY_<AUDIT_ID> -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# Allow loopback (in-container communication)
-A PVAS_BRIDGE_DENY_<AUDIT_ID> -i lo -j ACCEPT

# Allow DNS to public resolvers (otherwise even acme.sh would fail)
# Comment out if you want zero DNS resolution
-A PVAS_BRIDGE_DENY_<AUDIT_ID> -p udp --dport 53 -j ACCEPT
-A PVAS_BRIDGE_DENY_<AUDIT_ID> -p tcp --dport 53 -j ACCEPT

# Drop everything else
-A PVAS_BRIDGE_DENY_<AUDIT_ID> -j DROP